{
  "about": "Names TIOW and Blender read. Top-level Library folders are '<Creator> - <Mark or group>' (or 'Bits'). A file's part type comes from the nearest folder whose name matches a type or one of its aliases; trailing words like RawStl, Mirrored, Sergeant and Support are ignored. After changing partTypes, re-run: node apply-blender-bridge.mjs --library \"<this file>\" 'joints' says which socket each part type snaps onto when TIOW Inbox auto-snaps it: socket name, 'sided' (R/L taken from the part's name), 'fit' (snap with that fit type on the socket instead of the socket's own), 'mirrorLeft' (a right-hand part with no left fit is mirrored onto a left socket, and a '...-Mirrored' file counts as a left), 'rock' (first let it tilt up to that many degrees to sit flush), 'settle' (then slide along that socket axis until it touches, e.g. '-Z'), 'touch' (stop when that percent of its inside touches rather than at the first touch, so a knee cuff or a lug can sink in), 'sink' (then press in that far, mm), 'pushOut' (false: settle only closes a gap and never pushes the part off the model). Knee Pads and Shoulder Pads sit on exact three-point anchors (Shin, Pad), so they don't rock or settle. 'jointAliases' lists older socket names that mean the same joint. Blender reads this file directly; no need to re-run the patch for joints. 'spans' lists other sockets the part also covers: a two-handed weapon comes with both arms, so it snaps to Shoulder R and clears Shoulder L. 'mirrorSides': a part with a fit for one side only is mirrored onto the other (Mar Fil's jump-pack fins). A part's own .sockets.json can set its 'category', which wins over TIOW's type.",
